import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

## The storage package: a bucket made from a URL whose scheme is its backend (S3, files beside the
## server, the server's memory), keeping a file, a large one in parts, signed links, serving one,
## the errors, and how it works. Static: the samples are server/test/app/Samples.roc's, which the
## server's tests run on every backend.
meta = {
	title: "Storage",
	description: "The storage package for Roc Solid apps: files kept in a bucket whose URL says where (any S3, files beside the server, or the server's memory for tests), put, read, described and deleted, uploaded in parts, linked to with signed URLs, and served to the browser without passing through Roc.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "bucket", label: "A bucket" },
		{ id: "objects", label: "Keeping a file" },
		{ id: "parts", label: "A large file, in parts" },
		{ id: "signed", label: "Signed links" },
		{ id: "serve", label: "Serving one" },
		{ id: "errors", label: "Errors" },
		{ id: "how", label: "How it works" },
		{ id: "limits", label: "Not yet" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				kicker={[<>{"The ecosystem · "}</>, <code>packages/storage</code>]}
				title="Files, in S3 or beside the server"
			>
				Uploads, avatars, videos: files that don't belong in the database go in a bucket. This package keeps
				a file, reads it back whole or in part, uploads a large one in parts, hands out links the browser
				uploads or downloads with directly, and serves a file of any size, ranges included.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				The bucket can be any S3 (AWS, Cloudflare R2, or rustfs or MinIO on your machine), a directory beside
				the server, or the server's memory for tests. A URL picks which; your code is the same for all three.
				The package runs on the server only. The browser reaches the files through links and answers your
				server gives it.
			</p>
			<p>
				The samples come from <code>server/test/app/Samples.roc</code>, which the server platform's tests run
				on memory, on a directory, and on a real S3 bucket when one is configured.
			</p>

			<Heading id="bucket">A bucket</Heading>
			<p>Name the package in the server's header, beside the server platform:</p>
			<Code
				title="main.roc's header"
				source={"app [program] { pf: platform \"../server/platform/main.roc\", storage: \"../packages/storage/main.roc\" }"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
			<p>
				Then make a bucket from its URL, kept in the server's environment (here <code>STORAGE_URL</code>):
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_files.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_files.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>Storage.from_url</code> only reads the URL; it doesn't contact the bucket. An empty or unreadable
				URL is a <code>StorageErr</code> that says what is wrong. The URL's scheme picks the backend:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="STORAGE_URL"
				source={"s3://KEY:SECRET@127.0.0.1:9000/uploads?region=us-east-1&style=path&tls=no   # rustfs or MinIO\ns3://KEY:SECRET@<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com/uploads?region=auto       # R2\ns3://KEY:SECRET@s3.eu-west-1.amazonaws.com/uploads?region=eu-west-1          # AWS\nfile:data/uploads?secret=…                                                    # files beside the server\nmemory:                                                                       # the server's memory: tests"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
				plain={Bool.True}
			/>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>S3</strong> (<code>s3://</code>). The URL's path is the bucket's name. <code>region</code>
					is the region the keys sign for: <code>us-east-1</code> if left out, <code>auto</code> for R2.
					<code>style=path</code> puts the bucket in the request's path, as rustfs and MinIO want; without it,
					the bucket goes in the host name, as AWS and R2 prefer. <code>tls=no</code> uses plain
					<code>http</code>. Write any character the URL would misread as <code>%XX</code>. Any other
					parameter is refused, so a typo such as <code>stlye=path</code> fails at once.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>A directory</strong> (<code>file:data/uploads</code>, relative to where the server starts;
					created if missing). Each file is a real file at its key, written to a temporary file and then moved
					into place, so a crash never leaves half a file. Its content type and ETag are kept beside it, in
					<code>.storage/</code>. A directory suits an app on one server, as SQLite does
					(<a href={Paths.platform_database}>the database</a>); in production, put it on a volume.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Memory</strong> (<code>memory:</code>, or <code>memory:name</code> for a separate bucket).
					Gone when the server stops. Use it in tests, which then need no S3.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				A directory or memory URL may also give <code>secret</code>, the key for its
				<a href="#signed">signed links</a>. Without it, the server picks a random one at each start, and links
				given out before a restart stop working.
			</p>
			<p>
				<code>Storage.check!(bucket)</code> asks whether the bucket answers: for S3, that the keys work for
				this bucket; for a directory, that it can be written to; memory always answers. Call it from a health
				check, so a wrong URL shows before the first upload.
			</p>

			<Heading id="objects">Keeping a file</Heading>
			<p>
				A file in a bucket is an object, stored under a key: its name in the bucket, slashes included
				(<code>avatars/ada</code>). Its bytes are a <code>List(U8)</code>.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_avatar.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_avatar.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>{"Storage.put!(bucket, key, bytes, { content_type })"}</code> keeps a file under a key,
					replacing whatever was there.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>{"Storage.put_upload!(bucket, key, upload)"}</code> keeps a file a form sent, as
					<code>{"Server.file(request, \"photo\")"}</code> reads it (<a href={"${Paths.calls}#forms"}>forms</a>),
					with the browser's type.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>Storage.get!(bucket, key)</code> reads a file whole. From S3, a file over 64 MiB is refused;
					read it in pieces instead.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>{"Storage.get_range!(bucket, key, { offset, length })"}</code> reads a piece of a file and
					answers the file's whole size, so you can read a large one piece by piece.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>Storage.head!(bucket, key)</code> describes a file without reading it: its size, content type
					and ETag. For a file put whole, the ETag is its MD5, on every backend.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>Storage.delete!(bucket, key)</code> deletes a file. Deleting a missing file is
					<code>Ok</code>, so a cleanup can safely run twice.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				On a directory or memory bucket, a key must be a plain path, so it can never reach outside the
				directory. Refused: an empty key, an empty segment (a leading <code>/</code>, or <code>a//b</code>), a
				<code>.</code> or <code>..</code> segment, and a key that starts with <code>.storage</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="parts">A large file, in parts</Heading>
			<p>
				A video is too large for one request. A multipart upload sends it in numbered parts, then joins them:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_parts.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_parts.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<ol>
				<li><code>Storage.start_upload!</code> starts the upload and answers its id.</li>
				<li>
					<code>Storage.upload_part!</code> sends one part by its number (1 to 10,000) and answers the part's
					ETag. Sending a number again replaces that part.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>Storage.finish_upload!</code> joins the parts you list, in number order, into one file. A part
					listed with an ETag that isn't its own is refused, and a part you don't list is dropped. The file's
					ETag is the MD5 of the parts' MD5s and their count (<code>…-3</code>), as S3 makes it.
				</li>
			</ol>
			<p>
				<code>Storage.abort_upload!</code> drops an upload and its parts. On S3, every part but the last must
				be 5 MiB or more; S3 refuses a smaller one at the end with <code>EntityTooSmall</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="signed">Signed links</Heading>
			<p>
				A signed link lets the browser upload or download one file directly, without your server code in the
				middle. Anyone holding it can make that one request until it expires, so treat it as a secret.
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_upload_link.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_upload_link.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Storage.signed_url!(bucket, key, { method, expires_s })"}</code> makes a link for a
				<code>GET</code> or a <code>PUT</code> of that key, valid for <code>expires_s</code> seconds, from 1
				up to a week. Anything else is <code>BadExpiry</code>.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>On S3</strong> it is S3's own presigned URL. The browser talks to S3, so your server never
					holds the bytes. Only the host is signed, so a <code>PUT</code> may send any content type.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>On a directory or memory</strong> it is a link to your own app, at <code>/_storage</code>
					under its public URL. A route of yours answers it there (D-116) by calling the package's handler.
					Each link is signed, so the route needs no middleware of yours:
				</li>
			</ul>
			<Code title="routes/_storage/+server.roc" source={"import storage.Storage\n\n# The signed links of the bucket STORAGE_URL names.\nrespond! = |{ request, .. }| Storage.signed_route!(request, \"STORAGE_URL\")"} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The handler reads the bucket from the variable it is given (<code>STORAGE_URL</code> here). Before it
				serves the file (ranges included) or keeps the one sent, it checks the link's method, its expiry and
				its signature: an HMAC-SHA256 of the method, the key and the expiry, made with the bucket's
				<code>secret</code>. A link it didn't make, a changed one or an expired one gets a 403. A
				<code>PUT</code> through the route is an ordinary request body, so it is limited to 16 MiB unless the
				server's <code>--max-body</code> allows more; send larger files in parts. For an S3 bucket the route
				has nothing to answer: S3 answers its own links.
			</p>

			<Heading id="serve">Serving one</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_serve.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_serve.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>{"Storage.serve!(bucket, key, request, { content_type, cache_control })"}</code> answers a
				request with a file of any size. The bytes never pass through Roc:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					From S3, the answer is a forward: the server streams the file from S3 to the browser as it arrives
					(<a href={"${Paths.platform_http}#forward"}>a body from elsewhere</a>).
				</li>
				<li>From a directory or memory, the server sends the file it keeps (<code>Server.stored</code>).</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				The request's <code>Range</code> and <code>If-None-Match</code> headers are passed on, so a video
				player's range requests get 206s and a browser's cache gets its 304s. A missing file is a 404. An
				empty <code>content_type</code> sends the type the file was stored with, and an empty
				<code>cache_control</code> sends none. The headers come from the request record, so you can change
				them first: <code>serve_whole!</code> above drops the range to always send the whole file.
			</p>

			<Heading id="errors">Errors</Heading>
			<p>Each function's error type lists only the errors it can give:</p>
			<ul>
				<li><code>NotFound</code>: no such file, or no such upload.</li>
				<li>
					<code>{"S3Err({ status, code, message })"}</code>: S3 answered with an error, such as
					<code>AccessDenied</code>, <code>SignatureDoesNotMatch</code>, <code>NoSuchBucket</code>,
					<code>EntityTooSmall</code>, <code>InvalidPart</code> or <code>InvalidRange</code>.
					<code>Storage.said(refusal)</code> writes one as a line for the log.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>StorageErr(why)</code>: S3 didn't answer, or answered too late; a file couldn't be written;
					or the key isn't valid. <code>Storage.from_url</code> gives one too, for a missing or unreadable URL.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				A directory or memory bucket gives a <code>StorageErr</code> where S3 gives an <code>S3Err</code> (a
				part listed with the wrong ETag, say). A range that starts past a file's end is no bytes on a
				directory or memory, and <code>InvalidRange</code> on S3.
			</p>

			<Heading id="how">How it works</Heading>
			<p>
				For S3, the package signs each request itself with AWS Signature Version 4 (its <code>SigV4</code>
				module, checked against AWS's worked examples) and sends it with the server platform's
				<code>Http.send!</code> (<a href={"${Paths.platform_http}#out"}>asking another server</a>). Requests
				time out after 30 seconds, or 5 minutes for one that carries or reads a file. <code>put!</code> signs
				its body's SHA-256, so S3 refuses the file if anything changed on the way.
			</p>
			<p>
				For a directory or memory, the server's host keeps the files (the platform's <code>Blobs</code>
				module), reading and writing them on its threads for blocking work, so a call waiting on a file
				doesn't hold up other Roc calls. A multipart upload keeps its parts under <code>.uploads/</code> until
				it is finished or aborted.
			</p>
			<p>
				The full API, with each function's type, is in <code>packages/storage/README.md</code>.
			</p>

			<Heading id="limits">Not yet</Heading>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Whole files in memory:</strong> <code>put!</code> and <code>get!</code> hold the whole file
					in memory. Read a large one with <code>get_range!</code>, or send it to the browser with
					<code>serve!</code>.
				</li>
				<li><strong>No listing</strong> of a bucket's files, and no copying a file to another key.</li>
				<li><strong>A directory belongs to one server:</strong> two servers can't share one.</li>
				<li><strong>S3's static keys only:</strong> no session tokens.</li>
			</ul>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
